      <div class="entry">
        <div class="entry__date">2022–2026</div>
        <div class="entry__body">
          <p class="entry__title">Universidad EAFIT<span class="entry__title-sub">, B.Sc. in Biology</span></p>
          <p class="entry__meta entry__meta--small">GPA: 4.44 / 5.00</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="sec__title">Research Experience</h2>

      <div class="entry">
        <div class="entry__date">Aug 2025 – Jan 2026</div>
        <div class="entry__body">
          <p class="entry__title">Visiting Student Intern</p>
          <p class="entry__meta">
            <a href="https://ecsolab.com/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">The Evolution of Cells, Symbionts and Organelles Lab</a>,
            Dept. of Biological Sciences, Purdue University
          </p>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>
              Studied how the non-conventional osmotrophic yeasts <em>K. marxianus</em> and
              <em>V. polyspora</em> reorganize proteome investment under carbon limitation, within
              the project <em>Proteome Allocation Rules in Osmotrophic Eukaryotes</em>.
            </li>
            <li>
              Generated high-resolution growth curves across defined carbon regimes and supported
              proteomics workflows — sample preparation and integrative analysis linking molecular
              allocation to cellular physiology.
            </li>
            <li>
              Tested and calibrated an automated continuous-culture system (Pioreactor) for the
              lab's steady-state experiments.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="entry">
        <div class="entry__date">2024–2026</div>
        <div class="entry__body">
          <p class="entry__title">Undergraduate Thesis Researcher</p>
          <p class="entry__meta">Universidad EAFIT, Advisor: Prof. Nicolás Pinel Peláez, Ph.D.</p>
          <p class="entry__meta entry__meta--small">
            Thesis: <em>Cultivable Microbial Community Structure Along a Light Gradient in a Tropical
            Volcaniclastic Cave</em>
            <a href="https://hdl.handle.net/10784/38213" rel="noopener" target="_blank">[permanent link]</a>
          </p>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>
              Characterized the cultivable microbial fraction of the Organal San Antonio — a tropical
              volcaniclastic cave at ~2350 m a.s.l. in Támesis, Antioquia — across a light-defined
              gradient spanning Entrance, Transition, and Dark sectors.
            </li>
            <li>
              Found systematic shifts in cultivable abundance and composition across zonation (a
              reduced, distinct aphotic assemblage; a partly unique Transition community),
              establishing a first spatially resolved cultivable baseline for an organal-type
              pseudokarstic system.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

